{"id":"workloads-workload-api-policies","corpus":"k8s","title":"PodGroup Scheduling Policies","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/workload-api/policies/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"PodGroup Scheduling Policies"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the GenericWorkload feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"Every PodGroup must declare a scheduling policy in its spec.schedulingPolicy field. This policy dictates how the scheduler treats the collection of Pods in the group."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Policy types"},{"kind":"p","text":"The schedulingPolicy field supports two policy types: basic and gang. You must specify exactly one."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Basic policy"},{"kind":"p","text":"The basic policy instructs the scheduler to evaluate all Pods on a best-effort basis. Unlike the gang policy, a PodGroup using the basic policy is considered feasible regardless of how many of its Pods are currently schedulable."},{"kind":"p","text":"The primary reason to use the basic policy is to organize Pods into a group for better observability and management, while still evaluating them together within a single, atomic PodGroup scheduling cycle."},{"kind":"p","text":"This policy is suited for groups that do not require simultaneous startup but logically belong together, or to open the way for group-level constraints that do not imply \"all-or-nothing\" placement."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Gang policy"},{"kind":"p","text":"The gang policy enforces \"all-or-nothing\" scheduling. This is essential for tightly-coupled workloads where partial startup results in deadlocks or wasted resources."},{"kind":"p","text":"This can be used for Jobs or any other batch process where all workers must run concurrently to make progress."},{"kind":"p","text":"The gang policy requires a minCount field, which is the minimum number of Pods that must be schedulable simultaneously for the group to be feasible:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Setting policies via PodGroupTemplates"},{"kind":"p","text":"When using the Workload API, you define scheduling policies inside PodGroupTemplates. The workload controller copies the policy from the template into each PodGroup it creates, making the PodGroup self-contained. Changes to the Workload's templates only affect newly created PodGroups, not existing ones."},{"kind":"p","text":"For standalone PodGroups (created without a Workload), you set spec.schedulingPolicy directly on the PodGroup itself."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Policies in CompositePodGroups"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the CompositePodGroup feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"When the CompositePodGroup feature gate and the scheduling.k8s.io/v1alpha3 API group are enabled, CompositePodGroupTemplates in a Workload and the CompositePodGroup objects also declare a scheduling policy."},{"kind":"p","text":"While a scheduling policy in a PodGroup governs a collection of individual Pods, a CompositePodGroup scheduling policy governs its direct child groups (which can be both CompositePodGroup and PodGroup objects)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Policy types for CompositePodGroups"},{"kind":"p","text":"Similar to PodGroups, the spec.schedulingPolicy field of a CompositePodGroup supports two types:"},{"kind":"li","text":"basic: Child groups within the CompositePodGroup are evaluated and admitted independently."},{"kind":"li","text":"gang: Enforces multi-level all-or-nothing scheduling across child groups. The CompositePodGroup is schedulable only if at least minGroupCount child groups can be scheduled simultaneously."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Setting composite policies via templates"},{"kind":"p","text":"When using the Workload API, scheduling policies for CompositePodGroups are defined inside CompositePodGroupTemplates. Workload controllers copy the schedulingPolicy specified in the templates into each CompositePodGroup created at runtime. Unlike leaf PodGroupTemplates where minCount can be updated, minGroupCount in a CompositePodGroupTemplate is immutable."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What's next"},{"kind":"li","text":"See the PodGroup API for how policies are carried at runtime."},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn about the Workload API that defines PodGroupTemplates."},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about PodGroup scheduling."},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about the gang scheduling algorithm."},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn about the scheduling building blocks and the workloadbuilder library that controllers use to compile these policies."}],"lemmas":{"scheduling":["scheduling","schedule"],"schedulings":["scheduling","schedulings"],"schedules":["schedule","schedules"],"policies":["policy","policies"],"features":["feature","features"],"featuring":["featuring","feature"],"featured":["featured","feature"],"uses":["use","uses"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"administrators":["administrator","administrators"],"wills":["will","wills"],"willing":["willing","will"],"willed":["willed","will"],"would":["would","will"],"needs":["need","needs"],"needed":["needed","need"],"needing":["needing","need"],"enables":["enables","enable"],"enabling":["enabling","enable"],"relevants":["relevants","relevant"],"components":["component","components"],"sees":["see","sees"],"seeing":["seeing","see"],"saw":["saw","see"],"seen":["seen","see"],"disables":["disables","disable"],"disabled":["di